  header.setHelpText("SOURCE DOCUMENT:\nAbstract: We introduce a neural network with a recurrent attention model over a possibly large external memory. The architecture is a form of Memory Network (, 2015) but unlike the model in that work, it is trained end-to-end, and hence requires significantly less supervision during training, making it more generally applicable in realistic settings. It can also be seen as an extension of RNNsearch to the case where multiple computational steps (hops) are performed per output symbol. The flexibility of the model allows us to apply it to tasks as diverse as (synthetic) question answering and to language modeling. For the former our approach is competitive with Memory Networks, but with less supervision. For the latter, on the Penn TreeBank and Text8 datasets our approach demonstrates comparable performance to RNNs and LSTMs. In both cases we show that the key concept of multiple computational hops yields improved results.\nReferences: @cite_1: Neural machine translation is a recently proposed approach to machine translation. Unlike the traditional statistical machine translation, the neural machine translation aims at building a single neural network that can be jointly tuned to maximize the translation performance. The models proposed recently for neural machine translation often belong to a family of encoder-decoders and consists of an encoder that encodes a source sentence into a fixed-length vector from which a decoder generates a translation. In this paper, we conjecture that the use of a fixed-length vector is a bottleneck in improving the performance of this basic encoder-decoder architecture, and propose to extend this by allowing a model to automatically (soft-)search for parts of a source sentence that are relevant to predicting a target word, without having to form these parts as a hard segment explicitly. With this new approach, we achieve a translation performance comparable to the existing state-of-the-art phrase-based system on the task of English-to-French translation. Furthermore, qualitative analysis reveals that the (soft-)alignments found by the model agree well with our intuition. @cite_11: Inspired by recent work in machine translation and object detection, we introduce an attention based model that automatically learns to describe the content of images. We describe how we can train this model in a deterministic manner using standard backpropagation techniques and stochastically by maximizing a variational lower bound. We also show through visualization how the model is able to automatically learn to fix its gaze on salient objects while generating the corresponding words in the output sequence. We validate the use of attention with state-of-the-art performance on three benchmark datasets: Flickr8k, Flickr30k and MS COCO.\n\nSUMMARY TO EVALUATE:\n**Related Work** The proposed architecture, a recurrent\u2011attention memory network, is built on a rich body of research that both motivates and extends the neural\u2011memory paradigm. Below we discuss the most relevant prior work, highlighting how our approach differs from, and builds upon, these contributions. 1. **Memory\u2011augmented neural networks** *Neural Turing Machines* and *Neural Random\u2011Access Machines* (Neural\u2011Turing\u2011Machine, 2015) introduce the concept of a persistent external memory that the network can read from and write to while performing a sequence of \u201chops.\u201d These models, however, require a hand\u2011crafted controller that decides when to read or write, and they are trained with a large amount of supervision (e.g., a teacher forcing signal). In contrast, our recurrent\u2011attention memory network (RAMN) learns end\u2011to\u2011end, allowing the network to discover its own memory\u2011access policy without the need for a separate controller or extensive supervision. This makes the architecture more suitable for realistic settings where supervision is scarce. 2. **Attention mechanisms in sequence modelling** The attention\u2011based models introduced in the machine\u2011translation literature (Bahdanau et al., 2015) and in image\u2011captioning (Xu et al., 2015) have two key advantages: (i) they allow the model to focus on the most relevant parts of the input at each decoding step, and (ii) they provide a soft\u2011alignment signal that can be visualised and interpreted. Our recurrent\u2011attention mechanism incorporates these ideas by allowing the network to repeatedly \u201chop\u201d over the external memory, thereby soft\u2011aligning the memory content with the target output. This soft\u2011alignment is a natural extension of the attention mechanism used in neural\u2011machine translation and image\u2011captioning, and it is applied in a recurrent fashion to multiple hops per output symbol. 3. **End\u2011to\u2011end training of memory\u2011augmented models** The work on *RNNsearch* (Miller et al., 2016) and *Memory Networks* (Miller et al., 2015) demonstrates that recurrent neural networks can be trained to retrieve information from a large external memory. However, these models rely on a fixed\u2011length vector representation of the input and on a hard\u2011segment memory\u2011access policy. Our architecture replaces this with a recurrent attention mechanism that learns to retrieve information from the memory in a soft, differentiable manner. This reduces the need for supervision and improves the flexibility of the model, allowing it to perform multiple hops per output symbol. 4. **Benchmarking on language modelling and question answering** The evaluation of our model on Penn TreeBank, Text8, and");

