  header.setHelpText("SOURCE DOCUMENT:\nAbstract: In this paper we propose an algorithm for enumerating diagonal Latin squares of small order. It relies on specific properties of diagonal Latin squares to employ symmetry breaking techniques, and on several heuristic optimizations and bit arithmetic techniques to make use of computational power of state-of-the-art CPUs. Using this approach we enumerated diagonal Latin squares of order at most 9, and vertically symmetric diagonal Latin squares of order at most 10.\nReferences: @cite_9: The sudoku minimum number of clues problem is the following question: what is the smallest number of clues that a sudoku puzzle can have? For several years it had been conjectured that the answer is 17. We have performed an exhaustive computer search for 16-clue sudoku puzzles and did not find any, thus proving that the answer is indeed 17. In this article we describe our method and the actual search. As a part of this project, we developed a novel way to enumerate hitting sets. The hitting set problem is computationally hard; it is one of Karp\u2019s 21 classic NP-complete problems. A standard backtracking algorithm for finding hitting sets would not be fast enough to search for a 16-clue sudoku puzzle exhaustively, even at today\u2019s supercomputer speeds. To make an exhaustive search possible, we designed an algorithm that allowed us to efficiently enumerate hitting sets of a suitable size. @cite_20: It is known that solving the minimum Sudoku problem can be done by checking 5,472,730,538 essentially different Sudoku grids, which can be checked independently or in parallel. However, the program Checker, written by McGuire, requires about 311 thousand years on one-core CPU to check these grids completely, according to our experimental analysis. This paper proposes a new algorithm, named a disjoint minimal unavoidable set (DMUS) algorithm, to help solve the minimum Sudoku problem. Then, incorporate the algorithm into the program and further tuning the program code. In our experiment, the performance was greatly improved by a factor of 128.67. Hence, the improved program by us requires about 2417.4 years only. Thus, it becomes feasible and optimistic to solve this program using a volunteer computing system, such as BOINC.\n\nSUMMARY TO EVALUATE:\n**Related Work**\n\nThe study of Latin squares, especially the diagonal subclass, and the enumeration of combinatorial objects in the past decades have two major strands that we draw upon in our work: the development of efficient search algorithms for Sudoku\u2011style puzzles and the use of symmetry\u2011breaking and bit\u2011arithmetic techniques for Latin square generation. 1\u20133\n\n1. **Sudoku Minimum\u2011Clue Problem and Hitting Sets**  \n   The \u201cminimum\u2011clue\u201d Sudoku problem, which asks for the smallest number of clues a Sudoku grid can have, was a longstanding open question. 4,5 The authors of the Sudoku study performed exhaustive computer searches for 16\u2011clue grids, and notably did not find any, thereby proving that the answer is 17. 4 They also developed a novel hitting\u2011set enumeration algorithm, which is a hard combinatorial problem (Karp\u2019s 21 NP\u2011complete problems). 4 The hitting\u2011set approach used in Sudoku enumeration is closely related to the enumeration of diagonal Latin squares, because the constraints in a Sudoku grid can be mapped to the constraints in a Latin square with a particular pattern of diagonals. 5\n\n2. **Disjoint Minimal Unavoidable Set (DMUS) Algorithm**  \n   The DMUS algorithm introduced by McGuire et\u202fal. 6 is a technique that efficiently enumerates minimal unavoidable sets, which is a key step in solving the minimum\u2011clue Sudoku problem. 6 The algorithm\u2019s performance gains (128\u00d7 speed\u2011up, 2417\u2011year runtime on a volunteer\u2011computing platform) are comparable to the speed\u2011ups achieved by the bit\u2011arithmetic and symmetry\u2011breaking techniques in our algorithm for diagonal Latin squares. 6\n\n3. **State\u2011of\u2011the\u2011Art CPU\u2011Based Enumeration**  \n   The work of the authors in the Sudoku study and the DMUS paper, both heavily reliant on modern CPU clusters, exemplifies the power of parallel computation in combinatorial enumeration. 4,6 The same computational paradigm is used in our algorithm, which harnesses the latest CPUs to generate diagonal Latin squares of order up to 9 and vertically symmetric squares of order up\u2011to\u201110. 7\n\n4. **Previous Latin Square Enumeration Efforts**  \n   Prior to our work, only a handful of Latin squares of small order were enumerated. 8 The");

